WEBVTT

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:08.633
["Love In Suburbia"
by Danny Connors playing]

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:22.063
♪ Woo-hoo, woo-hoo, woo-hoo... ♪

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:24.065
[woman] Ben and I have been friends
for a year and a half.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:27.652
He's brilliantly smart.
He's compassionate.

00:00:27.736 --> 00:00:29.279
He is a diamond in the rough,

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:32.282
and I don't think women appreciate it.

00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:37.912
♪ All I wanna do... ♪

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:39.914
[man]
Ben is looking for the love of his life.

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:44.753
When women say that they want to meet
a funny, emotional, caring man...

00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:47.047
Ben is that person.

00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:52.594
♪ Is fall in love
In suburbia with you... ♪

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:56.181
[man] I've known been since he's moved
to New Orleans two years ago.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:01:00.268
He's a hopeless romantic
who has a great heart and great charisma.

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:03.146
In the modern dating scene,
those things are overlooked.

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:14.991
[woman] Ben is too good for this world.

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.868
He is the most dateable guy I know.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:20.413
-But truly, truly nice guys finish last.
-[exhales]

00:01:29.714 --> 00:01:31.424
-Hello.
-Hi.

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:32.592
-[Jaden] Hey.
-[chuckles]

00:01:33.176 --> 00:01:35.512
-It's nice to meet you.
-It's nice to meet you.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.597
[both laughing]

00:01:37.806 --> 00:01:39.390
I... I'm, uh...[stutters]

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:41.392
[laughing] Why not both?

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:42.977
-Okay, what's your name?
-I'm Ben.

00:01:43.061 --> 00:01:45.897
-Ben. I'm Jaden. It's nice to meet you.
-And it's nice to meet you.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.774
-I, uh...
-These are for me?

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:49.234
-They are for you.
-Okay.

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:51.319
I don't think
I've ever gotten flowers before.

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:55.990
-Well, hopefully, it's good. [chuckles]
-Yeah, it's a very good thing. Thank you.

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:58.493
I'm not 100% sure
what kind of flowers those are.

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:01.704
I think they might be chrysanthemums.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:03.540
So you brought me something
you don't know--

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:04.958
No, I'm just joking. [laughs]

00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:06.292
Only the finest.

00:02:06.876 --> 00:02:08.711
-Have you ever been here?
-I haven't.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.672
I haven't either. [chuckles]

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:14.217
-It's gonna be a great experience.
-It's gonna-- We don't know the flowers.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.512
We don't know the restaurant.
We don't know each other.

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:20.557
-I'm excited.
-[laughs] Riddles are great. We'll learn.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.559
-We'll learn all of these things.
-Yes.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.311
-You ready?
-Want to go in?

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:26.771
-Yeah, let's do it.
-Great.

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:28.690
-After you.
-Thank you.

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:34.696
[Ben] Do you want to get a drink
at the bar?

00:02:34.779 --> 00:02:35.655
Sure.

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:40.118
-Can I--Can I get your chair for you?
-Thank you.

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:44.414
-You're a gentleman.
-Oh, yeah. Well, what can I say? It was...

00:02:45.081 --> 00:02:46.374
mostly ready already.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:47.709
[both laughing]

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:55.466
What can I get for y'all?

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:59.262
Uh, may I please grab a Tito's and soda
with a splash of cranberry and lime?

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:00.638
-Of course.
-Thank you.

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:03.016
Um, I think I'll just have
a Dark 'n' Stormy.

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:04.225
A Dark 'n Stormy. Fantastic.

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:06.436
-Have that out in just a minute.
-Thank you so much.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.563
Easy order.

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:11.691
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I-- I'm... [stammers]

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:17.197
Prior to coming to New Orleans,
I was never much of a drinker.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.366
And I'm still kind of not much
of a drinker, if I'm being honest,

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.619
but this city has its way...

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.746
-of kind of pulling that out of you.
-Definitely does.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.460
I'm a huge closet nerd.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.587
-I love Dragon Ball Z.
-Okay, yeah.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.048
So, so, so, I... I...

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:40.637
[stammers] I confess that is
an acquired taste that I never acquired.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.347
I understand completely.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.767
Have you watched, um...
Avatar: The Last Airbender?

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.227
I feel like I watched it
a bazillion years ago

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:50.813
-and don't remember anything about it.
-Oh, Jaden, oh.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:51.856
I'll have to watch it.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.567
It is one of the greatest television shows
ever created.

00:03:55.652 --> 00:03:57.820
All right, tell me about yourself.
What do you do?

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.990
Okay. Uh, I am a professor...

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:02.242
-Really?
-...of computer science.

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:04.118
-Up at the University of New Orleans.
-Amazing.

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:05.036
Thank you.

00:04:05.662 --> 00:04:07.705
Yeah. So I'm a full-time student.

00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:10.041
-Mechanical engineering.
-Exciting!

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:11.918
Oh, that's awesome.
Where do you go to school?

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:14.504
Uh, to University of New Orleans.

00:04:15.338 --> 00:04:16.547
Which is nearby.

00:04:16.631 --> 00:04:18.925
-Mm-hmm.
-Yeah. Okay, great.

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:22.470
I'm... Uh, uh... I'm so sorry
for getting a weird look on my face.

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:26.307
I... [stammers] I am a professor
at the University of New Orleans.

00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:27.225
-Oh, are you?
-Yeah.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:28.518
Nice. [chuckles]

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:29.477
Yeah.

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:31.521
So I...

00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:34.983
I am not sure if we are allowed...

00:04:35.483 --> 00:04:37.819
-to be here with each other right now.
-[chuckling]

00:04:39.028 --> 00:04:39.904
But you're not...

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:41.406
my student.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.866
-You are not my professor.
-Yeah.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.953
I have no power over what grades you have.

00:04:47.036 --> 00:04:49.872
-Yes.
-I don't know any of your professors.

00:04:51.332 --> 00:04:54.335
-I think that we should be okay.
-I think we should be okay.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.088
-[Shamerick] This is exciting.
-[Ben] Yeah.

00:04:57.171 --> 00:04:58.131
Cheers.

00:04:58.214 --> 00:05:00.300
-[chuckles] Cheers.
-Cheers to a good night.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:01.718
-To a good night.
-Yes.

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:06.723
-Have you ever been here before?
-Uh, uh, uh, just once or twice.

00:05:06.806 --> 00:05:08.599
I've played a little jazz trio gig.

00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:09.851
-No way!
-Yeah.

00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:11.769
-That's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.273
[stammers] Uh, I... What...

00:05:15.356 --> 00:05:18.067
Did you... did you sing?
Did you play an instrument?

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.694
Yeah, I sang and played guitar,

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:22.655
and had an upright bass player
and saxophone, so...

00:05:22.739 --> 00:05:25.158
That's incredible. I, uh...

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:28.369
I certainly don't want
to misrepresent myself,

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:34.083
but, uh, in my youth, like growing up,
I took a lot of singing classes and--

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:35.335
-Really?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:35.418 --> 00:05:36.794
-Did you do musicals?
-I did.

00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:38.296
-You did musicals?
-I did, yes!

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:39.714
-Yes, I did.
-My gosh, me too.

00:05:39.797 --> 00:05:42.050
-Oh, my gosh.
-We should go audition together.

00:05:42.216 --> 00:05:43.676
-I would totally--
-Would you?

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.594
-Yeah.
-Would you?

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.012
-I think...
-Are you just saying--

00:05:46.095 --> 00:05:47.180
No, I am not saying that.

00:05:47.263 --> 00:05:51.142
So I think there's, um, a production
coming up of Oliver!

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.936
Yeah, yeah, yeah!

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:57.482
So I was a dancer. I danced in college.

00:05:57.565 --> 00:05:59.442
-More for fun.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:02.278
-I was a writer for a magazine here.
-No way.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:03.988
I have modeled here for many years.

00:06:04.072 --> 00:06:05.698
Well, that makes sense.

00:06:05.782 --> 00:06:09.118
Then I got into real estate
and I love the autonomy and...

00:06:09.827 --> 00:06:10.745
What... [stammers]

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:12.246
-The autonomy.
-Autonomy, sorry.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.165
Sorry. No, yeah, yeah, um, uh...

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:15.083
The accent.

00:06:15.166 --> 00:06:16.376
Oh, no, no. [laughing]

00:06:16.459 --> 00:06:19.587
-Is that a Mississippi twang? [chuckles]
-Southern thing, yes.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.255
And I'm-- I'm a California boy.

00:06:21.339 --> 00:06:22.215
Really?

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:24.550
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-I am not from California.

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:26.636
-Okay. Okay.
-I'm from Prairieville.

00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:29.931
-I don't know what that is.
-Uh, it is exactly how it sounds.

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:31.849
It is Hicksville, USA.

00:06:31.933 --> 00:06:34.685
Um, so I was born in Lafayette.

00:06:34.769 --> 00:06:36.771
So you're... you're from New Orleans.

00:06:36.854 --> 00:06:38.815
-I would say so, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:39.941
Yeah, born and raised.

00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:40.983
-[Ben] No way?
-Yeah.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:43.444
I actually have, uh, two homes, so I--

00:06:43.528 --> 00:06:45.571
-You have two homes?
-Well, my... [laughing]

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:48.908
-That's...
-Yeah. So I go back and forth...

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:51.494
um, because, um, I have children.

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:52.537
[gasps]

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.621
-Yeah.
-Cool.

00:06:53.704 --> 00:06:55.289
-I have two boys.
-Okay.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:56.207
-Yeah.
-Awesome.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.584
Uh, um...

00:06:58.668 --> 00:07:01.587
-Do you have any children?
-I do not. No.

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:02.630
Do you want kids?

00:07:02.713 --> 00:07:05.049
-[stuttering]
-[chuckling]

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:08.469
With your career path, is that in it?

00:07:08.553 --> 00:07:10.805
So-- Man. Okay.

00:07:10.888 --> 00:07:12.431
Um, I...

00:07:13.683 --> 00:07:16.436
[stammers]
Like, the short answer is "yes."

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.522
[waiter]
Hey, guys. I've got your table ready.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.274
-Right over here.
-Perfect. Great.

00:07:21.691 --> 00:07:23.067
Uh, I guess...

00:07:25.153 --> 00:07:27.822
[chuckles] So sorry.
It's a delicate operation.

00:07:28.322 --> 00:07:29.615
Can I get your drink for you?

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:30.741
-Please.
-Yeah.

00:07:31.534 --> 00:07:32.869
-[waiter] Right over here.
-Great.

00:07:46.382 --> 00:07:48.342
-[scoffs] I'm starving.
-Me too.

00:07:48.426 --> 00:07:50.136
-Me too.
-Oh, Perfect timing.

00:07:50.219 --> 00:07:51.679
-[waiter] Hey, guys. Welcome.
-Hi.

00:07:51.762 --> 00:07:53.097
Thank you. [chuckles]

00:07:53.264 --> 00:07:56.601
Thank you so much. It's beautiful.
[laughing]

00:07:57.059 --> 00:07:58.895
[singing indistinctly, inhales]

00:07:58.978 --> 00:07:59.937
Uh...

00:08:00.021 --> 00:08:01.772
-You're funny.
-You're funny.

00:08:02.273 --> 00:08:03.107
Okay. [laughing]

00:08:03.191 --> 00:08:05.443
Oh, would you not like to be funny?

00:08:05.526 --> 00:08:07.778
-No, that's cool. I can be funny.
-Ah. Great.

00:08:08.279 --> 00:08:10.072
I'm definitely going with the gnocchi.

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:11.157
Yes, I am too.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.658
I love the gnocchi.

00:08:13.242 --> 00:08:15.786
-That's what I want too.
-Okay, perfect! Great.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.580
It's one thing we have in common,
for sure.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:19.916
Good. Yeah. [laughs] Thank goodness.

00:08:19.999 --> 00:08:21.250
I was just kidding.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:24.879
[Ben] Um...

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:28.925
So, I...

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:30.384
I'm curious. Like...

00:08:30.468 --> 00:08:31.594
-Okay. About?
-Like...

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:33.721
Do you go on dates often?

00:08:33.804 --> 00:08:36.432
I do. Uh, well, actually, not recently.

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:38.726
I kind of took a break
from the dating scene,

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:42.271
-because it's, uh, exhausting.
-Yeah! [laughing]

00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:43.814
-[Alex] I work a lot.
-Yes.

00:08:44.524 --> 00:08:47.944
So it doesn't leave a lot of time
for dating or meeting people.

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:49.111
Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:49.737 --> 00:08:51.906
Yeah, it's been interesting having...

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:56.077
having been engaged for, like,
two or more years.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.286
Yeah, it's scary.

00:08:57.828 --> 00:09:00.414
-Especially at this age.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:00.498 --> 00:09:02.166
-Going back into--
-Getting back out there.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.961
Most people are married with children.
On their second marriage.

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:06.921
Uh-huh! And there's that sense of like,

00:09:07.004 --> 00:09:10.049
"Oh, my gosh,
I have these things that I--"

00:09:10.132 --> 00:09:11.300
Clock is ticking.

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:12.593
Uh-huh! Uh-huh!

00:09:13.177 --> 00:09:14.804
-It's daunting.
-It's daunting.

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:16.097
It's terrifying.

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.560
So what about you?
Tell me something about...

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:23.312
Tell me-- Tell you something about me?

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:26.774
Tell me something about yourself
that I don't know.

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:29.360
-Which could be anything.
-Okay, anything. Whoo, whoo, uh...

00:09:29.443 --> 00:09:32.572
-[chuckles]
-Well, I have...

00:09:33.114 --> 00:09:38.411
uh, basically not dated at all
in some ways.

00:09:38.494 --> 00:09:39.328
-I...
-Okay.

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:41.372
I've had, uh...

00:09:41.455 --> 00:09:43.749
Uh... one of the first...

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:46.252
and only women that I ever dated...

00:09:46.877 --> 00:09:51.841
Uh, we were dating
for about ten and a half years.

00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:52.967
-[Kat] Okay.
-Yeah.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:53.968
-Yeah, yeah...
-Feel ya.

00:09:54.051 --> 00:09:55.011
Yeah. Yeah?

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.388
-Eight and a half.
-Okay.

00:09:57.471 --> 00:09:59.307
-Yeah.
-Ended in March.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:02.977
Okay. So you're still
kind of fresh off of that?

00:10:03.561 --> 00:10:08.524
I mean-- I didn't--
Like, everyone's process is different.

00:10:09.191 --> 00:10:10.735
[Shamerick] It's time to live.

00:10:10.818 --> 00:10:11.777
It is time to live.

00:10:11.861 --> 00:10:14.655
We've been in these relationships
for a long time.

00:10:14.739 --> 00:10:16.157
-Yeah.
-And now I feel like...

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:20.494
I don't know. Benjamin Button, but, like,
backwards, reversed. You know what I mean?

00:10:20.578 --> 00:10:24.457
Wait, wait. Benjamin Button reversed
is just normal. [laughs]

00:10:24.749 --> 00:10:27.168
Okay, right. Yeah, you're right.
I'm sorry.

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:29.754
-Look, forget that part.
-[laughs]

00:10:29.837 --> 00:10:30.671
The food's here.

00:10:30.755 --> 00:10:32.506
Okay. Perfect. Perfect timing.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:34.216
-This looks delicious.
-Thank you so much.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.804
I'm slow. Don't wait for me.
Feel free to dig in.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.056
[chuckles]

00:10:48.356 --> 00:10:49.190
[giggles]

00:10:50.566 --> 00:10:51.525
Thank you.

00:10:52.777 --> 00:10:54.236
-I love gnocchi.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:55.696
Hard to go wrong.

00:10:57.573 --> 00:10:59.659
Um, I...

00:11:00.451 --> 00:11:04.038
So, this is the first blind date

00:11:04.121 --> 00:11:06.624
-that you've ever been on?
-Oh, yeah. Yeah. Absolutely.

00:11:06.707 --> 00:11:08.417
-A hundred percent.
-I think it's going very well.

00:11:08.501 --> 00:11:10.086
-I know. I like it.
-Blind dates...

00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:12.588
-Not too bad.
-Not too shabby.

00:11:12.672 --> 00:11:15.966
Um, so you have...

00:11:16.467 --> 00:11:17.385
a family.

00:11:17.468 --> 00:11:18.886
You've got a couple of kids.

00:11:18.969 --> 00:11:22.056
Are you interested
in having any more kids?

00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:23.557
I am actually.

00:11:23.641 --> 00:11:26.477
It shocks people, 'cause I'm like,
"Look, I'm open to whatever."

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.354
-You never know what the future...
-Yeah.

00:11:28.437 --> 00:11:29.313
...is gonna hold.

00:11:29.397 --> 00:11:32.400
I'm either going to never have kids
or I'm going to adopt.

00:11:33.567 --> 00:11:34.819
-Interesting. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:11:34.902 --> 00:11:37.571
Growing up in Prairieville, Louisiana,
you go on a date with a guy

00:11:37.655 --> 00:11:39.532
and you tell him you don't want kids?
Oh, my God.

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:40.908
Smoke trail.

00:11:41.033 --> 00:11:42.410
Oh... [stuttering] the Road Runner?

00:11:42.493 --> 00:11:45.204
"You don't want my spawn?"
'Cause God forbid,

00:11:45.287 --> 00:11:48.207
everybody in Prairieville thinks
they have, you know, the golden seed.

00:11:48.332 --> 00:11:50.209
[laughs] I'm sorry.

00:11:50.292 --> 00:11:52.670
That's wildly inappropriate. [chuckles]

00:11:52.753 --> 00:11:55.840
Um... [chuckles, inhales] Uh...

00:11:55.923 --> 00:11:56.757
No comment.

00:11:56.841 --> 00:11:58.259
No comment on my seed.

00:11:58.384 --> 00:12:01.470
[laughing]

00:12:02.847 --> 00:12:04.265
Um...

00:12:08.811 --> 00:12:10.271
[Stephanie] New Orleans is beautiful.

00:12:10.354 --> 00:12:13.899
And I'm-- And I'm so proud to be--

00:12:13.983 --> 00:12:16.277
To call this place home. I really am.

00:12:17.027 --> 00:12:20.531
What... What was it like
living through Katrina?

00:12:20.614 --> 00:12:23.242
Um, I was still fairly young
when it happened.

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:26.454
I can't say, like,
I have too many memories.

00:12:26.537 --> 00:12:29.582
Maybe, for some reason,
you just block some memories out.

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:31.208
Yeah. It's...

00:12:31.876 --> 00:12:36.046
amazing, the scar it's left on the city.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:37.006
Definitely.

00:12:37.506 --> 00:12:40.092
[stammers] But also,
any other city in the world,

00:12:40.176 --> 00:12:42.720
if that happened to it,
it would just be gone.

00:12:42.803 --> 00:12:44.388
But there's something about...

00:12:44.972 --> 00:12:48.893
this city that just inspires people
to, like, cling on.

00:12:48.976 --> 00:12:51.312
A city is just a bunch of buildings,
right?

00:12:51.395 --> 00:12:54.315
But, like, until you fill it with people.

00:12:55.024 --> 00:12:57.651
And I guess-- I just-- Again, I'm...

00:12:57.902 --> 00:13:01.947
Wow, yeah. It's weird that I'm getting
emotional. I'm getting worked up about it.

00:13:02.031 --> 00:13:06.243
I think that the people of New Orleans
are absolutely incredible.

00:13:06.327 --> 00:13:08.954
Well, we're glad to have you. [laughs]

00:13:09.038 --> 00:13:10.915
Thank you. [laughs]

00:13:11.457 --> 00:13:16.170
Um, uh, I would love to know
just, like, what you like to do for fun?

00:13:16.253 --> 00:13:18.881
Actually, I just recently started

00:13:18.964 --> 00:13:20.841
-hosting a radio show.
-No way?

00:13:20.925 --> 00:13:24.470
It's like a musicians and artists
mental wellness type show.

00:13:24.553 --> 00:13:26.847
But music that we find healing.

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:28.182
That is incredible. I...

00:13:28.265 --> 00:13:30.309
I asked you what you do for fun

00:13:30.392 --> 00:13:34.313
and what you do for fun is help other
people be the best that they can be.

00:13:34.396 --> 00:13:37.525
-Didn't think about it like that.
-That's amazing.

00:13:38.526 --> 00:13:39.527
Uh...

00:13:40.277 --> 00:13:41.153
I'm having a really...

00:13:41.862 --> 00:13:43.781
-awesome time with you.
-I am too.

00:13:43.864 --> 00:13:46.575
Do you maybe want to, like...

00:13:46.659 --> 00:13:48.452
-Go to another place?
-...go to a--

00:13:48.536 --> 00:13:49.954
-Get some drinks or something?
-Sure.

00:13:50.037 --> 00:13:51.622
Wait, I can't leave my flowers.

00:13:51.705 --> 00:13:54.041
I'm touched. [laughs]

00:13:54.667 --> 00:13:56.544
You thought I would forget? Oh, no.

00:13:57.127 --> 00:13:59.547
-[Kat] Shall we?
-I think we shall. [laughing]

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:16.188
So, do you have any favorite places
in New Orleans that you go to?

00:14:16.313 --> 00:14:18.232
-Uh, uh, yeah.
-Like what?

00:14:18.315 --> 00:14:20.025
Actually, have you ever been to...

00:14:20.317 --> 00:14:22.820
Uh... Ooh, ooh...

00:14:22.903 --> 00:14:25.030
Lebanon Cafe? Cafe Lebanon?

00:14:25.114 --> 00:14:27.157
No, but that's one of my favorite
types of food, so...

00:14:27.241 --> 00:14:28.951
-Great.
-Gonna have to keep that in mind.

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:30.286
Yeah. Well, maybe...

00:14:30.369 --> 00:14:32.246
-[mumbling]
-[chuckling]

00:14:33.914 --> 00:14:35.958
Um... So...

00:14:36.458 --> 00:14:38.210
I had...

00:14:38.711 --> 00:14:41.505
a really incredible time.

00:14:42.006 --> 00:14:44.800
Uh, but I... I also...

00:14:46.135 --> 00:14:47.094
[stammers] I, uh...

00:14:47.636 --> 00:14:51.974
Even though it's probably totally fine
and there's nothing to worry about,

00:14:52.057 --> 00:14:56.437
I'm a professor, you're a student,
we're at the same university.

00:14:57.479 --> 00:14:58.314
Before...

00:14:58.689 --> 00:15:01.901
we see each other again, if you even
wanted to see each other again...

00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:03.652
I would feel more comfortable

00:15:03.736 --> 00:15:07.114
